"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[296],{2819:(e,a,t)=>{t.d(a,{A:()=>i});var n=t(9575);function i(e){return(0,n.jsx)("a",{...e})}},7110:(e,a,t)=>{t.d(a,{OZ:()=>s,Tj:()=>o});let n=JSON.parse('[{"slug":"formal-state","title":"Formal state representation and transition semantics","category":"Foundations","level":"Essential","description":"Build a precise model of the board, ordered body, food, and legal transitions.","diagram":"state","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"Start with a ruleset","paragraphs":["Snake is a family of games. A proof about one member need not hold for another. Our reference model uses a finite rectangular grid, four-neighbor movement, solid boundaries, one food cell, and immediate growth by one cell when food is eaten. There is no starvation timer. Obstacles, when present, are fixed unless a rule explicitly changes them.","Let the board be V = {0,…,W−1} \xd7 {0,…,H−1}. A body B is an ordered sequence of distinct vertices, starting at the head and ending at the tail. Consecutive vertices share a grid edge. A set of occupied cells cannot substitute for this sequence: it tells you where the body is, but not which cell leaves next."],"formula":"s = (B, d, f, O, ξ),    B = (b₀, b₁, …, bℓ₋₁)"},{"heading":"What belongs in the state","paragraphs":["The direction d determines which reversal is prohibited. The food location f affects growth, and O stores obstacles. The symbol ξ represents the pseudorandom generator state when exact replay matters. An environment with queued growth, health, timed walls, or simultaneous opponents needs additional variables.","For a stochastic model, the transition law may sample food without exposing the generator state. For an exact implementation replay, the generator state or the full sequence of random outcomes must be retained. These are distinct modeling goals. “Markov state” means sufficient information for the transition distribution, not necessarily access to hidden random bits."]},{"heading":"Derive a transition","paragraphs":["An action proposes a neighboring head cell q. Reject the action if it leaves the board or enters an obstacle. Determine whether q contains food before deciding which body cells block it. On a growing move, all current body cells remain occupied; on a non-growing move, the old tail is removed.","The next body is formed by prepending q, retaining the tail only on a growing move. This convention allows entry into the old tail on a non-growing step. A game that checks all collisions before removing the tail implements a different transition function. Neither convention should be silently assumed."],"formula":"B′ = (q, b₀, …, bℓ₋₂)  without food;    B′ = (q, b₀, …, bℓ₋₁)  with food"},{"heading":"Why the distinction matters","paragraphs":["Two states can have the same occupied set and different body order. Their next vacant cells can differ, so an occupancy-only planner may give them the same action even when their legal continuations differ. This is a state-aliasing problem, not a weakness of a particular search algorithm.","The practical consequence is a disciplined separation: an image or feature vector is an observation, while the simulator maintains the complete state. Any claim that an observation is sufficient must be justified by the selected rules. The equations here specify SnakeGuide’s educational model; the linked Battlesnake rules document a different, concrete implementation."]}],"takeaway":"Write the transition function before choosing the planner. Body order and update order are part of the problem.","sources":["gym-env","battlesnake-rules"],"abstract":"A Snake configuration is an ordered body embedded in a board, together with food, direction, and any ruleset-specific state. This note specifies legal transitions under immediate growth and solid boundaries, then distinguishes the simulator state from the information available in a rendered board. The model provides a common vocabulary for planning, collision detection, and reproducible evaluation."},{"slug":"tail-movement","title":"Tail release, growth, and collision constraints","category":"Foundations","level":"Essential","description":"Understand vacating cells, growth, and the off-by-one errors that change legal moves.","diagram":"state","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"A collision check is a timing decision","paragraphs":["Consider the four-cell body [(3,3),(2,3),(2,2),(3,2)], listed head first. Moving upward proposes (3,2), the current tail. In our immediate-growth model, this is legal if that cell is not food: the tail vacates during the same transition. Checking membership in the whole old body would reject a legal move.","The opposite error is to delete the tail unconditionally before evaluating growth. If a game permits food or a growth event at the destination, the tail may need to remain. Collision and growth logic must agree on the body that persists into the next state."]},{"heading":"Release times are conditional","paragraphs":["Suppose the current length is ℓ and growth never occurs. The segment initially at index i, counting the head as zero, leaves the body after ℓ−i moves. The initial tail leaves after one move. A release-time map can therefore show when initially occupied cells might become available.","This map is conditional on the future. Eating delays releases. Newly visited head cells become occupied even though they were empty in the initial map. A planner that records only initial release times may accept a route that crosses its own newly created body."],"formula":"release(bᵢ) = ℓ − i    under no growth and continued legal movement"},{"heading":"A moving target is not a safety proof","paragraphs":["Searching for a route to the current tail is a useful heuristic because the tail often opens space. But a static path to that cell is not necessarily a route to the tail’s future position. Every intermediate move changes the geometry and may change which escape routes remain available.","A stronger check simulates a proposed route using the real transition function, then evaluates the resulting body. Even this proves only what was checked: successful execution of a finite prefix does not imply indefinite survival, and a static continuation check does not prove a dynamic continuation."]},{"heading":"Implement the boundary cases","paragraphs":["Test entering the vacating tail, entering a non-tail segment, eating at the boundary, filling the final free cell, and requesting an immediate reversal. These cases reveal semantic disagreements that a high average score can conceal.","The diagram toggles an exact non-growing transition in the four-cell example. The playable lab uses this same convention. Battlesnake’s documented growth uses tail duplication, so do not import its timing assumptions into this engine without translating the rules."]}],"takeaway":"A cell is not simply occupied or empty: its availability depends on transition timing and growth.","sources":["battlesnake-rules","gym-env"],"abstract":"Collision legality depends on the order of movement and growth. We analyze entry into a vacating tail cell, derive conditional release times for body segments, and identify cases in which a static occupancy test rejects a legal move. The accompanying transition figure computes both a non-growing tail entry and a growing move from the same ordered body."},{"slug":"wall-mode","title":"Boundary conditions and wall-mode topology","category":"Foundations","level":"Intermediate","description":"Separate boundary walls, fixed obstacles, and walls that appear during play.","diagram":"bottleneck","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"Three different meanings of wall","paragraphs":["A solid boundary forbids leaving the rectangle. An interior obstacle removes a vertex or forbids traversal through it. A dynamic wall changes the available graph during play. These mechanisms are often grouped under “wall mode,” but they create different planning problems.","Our pathfinding lab uses editable interior obstacles that remain fixed during a search. It is not a reproduction of a proprietary game’s wall-spawning rules. An analysis of a particular game needs its exact version, placement process, timing, and collision convention."]},{"heading":"Separate geometry from body occupancy","paragraphs":["Let G be the four-neighbor board graph. Removing obstacle vertices gives the traversable terrain graph. Removing the body, subject to the tail convention, gives a momentary free-space graph. The terrain may stay fixed while the free-space graph changes at every move.","This distinction explains why two visually similar bottlenecks behave differently. A passage blocked by a fixed wall never opens under the current rules. A passage blocked by the tail may open on the next move. A component analysis must identify which of these graphs it analyzes."],"formula":"Gterrain = G[V ∖ O]    and    Gsnapshot = G[V ∖ (O ∪ Bblocked)]"},{"heading":"Specify dynamic wall events","paragraphs":["A wall-spawning model needs a candidate set, a sampling distribution, an event trigger, and exclusions. For example, “spawn after each food” leaves unanswered whether the new food, body, and essential passages can be covered. A connectivity-preserving sampler defines a different task from unrestricted sampling.","If wall placement is stochastic, a policy’s safety can be probabilistic even when its movement is deterministic. A guarantee based on one fixed Hamiltonian cycle expires when an inserted wall removes a vertex on that cycle. Replanning is an additional obligation, not a consequence of the old proof."]},{"heading":"A useful position taxonomy","paragraphs":["Describe a position by traversable components, narrow passages, body occupancy, head and tail placement, and the available exit directions. Then distinguish immediate illegal moves, finite-horizon traps, and states merely rejected by a heuristic. These labels carry different evidential strength.","A practical case study should retain the full ordered body and wall set, show the failed action, and replay the transition. Calling a state “unavoidable” requires a proof or exhaustive analysis under a precisely bounded ruleset. A failed agent run alone establishes only that the tested agent failed."]}],"takeaway":"Wall geometry, body timing, and wall-generation rules must be specified independently.","sources":["nx-cut","battlesnake-ruleset","itai-1982"],"abstract":"The phrase wall mode can denote solid boundaries, fixed internal obstacles, or obstacles introduced during play. These cases produce different transition systems. We separate board geometry from temporary body occupancy, describe how dynamic events alter reachability, and organize positions by constraints that can be inspected without assuming a universal game implementation."},{"slug":"flood-fill","title":"Reachable-space heuristics and their limitations","category":"Algorithms","level":"Intermediate","description":"Use flood fill as a diagnostic for confinement—and understand what it cannot certify.","diagram":"bottleneck","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"Turn open cells into a component","paragraphs":["A flood fill starts at a cell and repeatedly visits each traversable neighbor. On a grid, a queue gives breadth-first traversal and a stack gives depth-first traversal. Both recover the same connected component when they use the same obstacle set.","To evaluate a candidate Snake move, first simulate that move. Then construct a snapshot of blocked cells and flood from the new head. This ordering matters: evaluating the old board can miss the new head’s obstruction or a tail cell that has just become free."]},{"heading":"Use the count with care","paragraphs":["A component with many open cells gives the head room to maneuver. A small component is a warning about confinement. One common heuristic compares reachable cells with body length, but the comparison depends on whether the head is counted and which body cells were removed from the graph.","Even a region larger than the body can have a dead end or a narrow passage that prevents safe traversal. Conversely, a small frozen region may expand as the tail moves. The count is neither a universal sufficient condition nor a universal necessary condition for eventual survival."],"formula":"R(s) = |reachable vertices from the head in the chosen snapshot graph|"},{"heading":"Distinguish connectivity from route structure","paragraphs":["A connected component guarantees a path between pairs of its vertices in the static graph. It does not guarantee a simple route visiting every vertex, a closed route, or an order compatible with a moving body. Those are stronger properties.","For example, a chamber attached through a one-cell neck can contribute many cells to the count. Entering it may still seal the exit behind the head. Articulation analysis exposes this neck; simulation reveals when the body might release it. Neither should be replaced by an unexplained threshold."]},{"heading":"Make it an interpretable baseline","paragraphs":["A transparent controller can reject immediate collisions, compute a flood-fill score for each remaining move, and use food distance to break ties. Document the score ordering and neighbor tie-break so the behavior can be reproduced. Such a controller is a baseline, not an optimal policy.","Measure failures by geometry: cul-de-sac entry, premature closure, starvation when applicable, and collision after growth. This turns a scalar score into a useful diagnostic. The article’s reasoning is an application of graph connectivity; the sources provide the underlying traversal and cut-vertex definitions."]}],"takeaway":"Reachable area measures a static component. Survival depends on how the body can move through it.","sources":["nx-bfs","nx-cut"],"abstract":"Flood fill measures the size of a connected free-space component in a frozen board. We explain its linear graph-search cost and show why the count does not certify that a moving snake can occupy or leave that region. Connectivity, bottlenecks, body order, and future growth must be considered separately when interpreting a space heuristic."},{"slug":"bottlenecks","title":"Articulation points and dynamic escape routes","category":"Algorithms","level":"Intermediate","description":"Read the board through cut vertices and the timing of a return path.","diagram":"bottleneck","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"Identify the hinge","paragraphs":["An articulation vertex is a vertex whose removal increases the number of connected components. In a grid, it can represent the only passage between two chambers. The definition is about a particular graph; always state whether that graph includes the body as blocked terrain.","The figure shows a wall dividing two regions except for one opening. Removing the opening disconnects the terrain graph. Occupying it with the body may temporarily have the same effect, but the timing of that occupation matters."]},{"heading":"A pocket is a geometric description","paragraphs":["A pocket is an informal name for a region with restricted access. Its danger depends on the entrance width, internal routes, food position, body length, and how long the entrance remains occupied. A large pocket can be dangerous, while a small one can be harmless to a short snake.","Before entering, ask where the head can turn, where the tail will be, and whether the body blocks the only return route. A one-cell corridor is especially restrictive because a body longer than one cannot simply reverse through its neck."]},{"heading":"Static decomposition, dynamic reasoning","paragraphs":["Cut vertices can be found by a depth-first traversal that tracks discovery times and low-link values. For a graph with V vertices and E edges, standard articulation analysis takes O(V+E) time. On a four-neighbor grid, E grows linearly with V.","The decomposition helps prioritize danger, but a cut in the current free-space graph may disappear after a tail release. Conversely, a move can create a new cut by occupying an open cell. Recompute or update the graph after simulating the candidate action instead of treating its topology as permanent."]},{"heading":"A defensible escape test","paragraphs":["One conservative procedure is to simulate a food route, inspect its terminal state, and reject it if a separately specified continuation test fails. This makes the criterion reviewable: the route simulation, graph construction, and continuation search can each be inspected.","It is still possible to reject safe moves or accept moves that fail beyond the searched horizon. Report the horizon and the abstraction. Reserve “trap” for a demonstrable claim such as “all legal action sequences die within k steps”; use “risk” for a geometric warning without that proof."]}],"takeaway":"A bottleneck marks a dependence on one passage. Whether it becomes fatal is a question about time and body order.","sources":["nx-cut","nx-bfs"],"abstract":"Removing a cut vertex disconnects a static graph, but the occupied cells of Snake change over time. This note relates articulation points to pockets and return paths, then separates a geometric warning from a survival guarantee. An interactive figure recomputes reachable cells when the only corridor between two board regions is closed."},{"slug":"space-time","title":"Space-time planning with endogenous occupancy","category":"Algorithms","level":"Advanced","description":"Move from static cells to timed states without losing the snake’s history.","diagram":"state","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"A route through changing occupancy","paragraphs":["Ordinary grid search uses a cell as a vertex. If an obstacle moves, reaching that cell at time three can differ from reaching it at time eight. A space-time graph therefore represents a vertex as (cell, time) and connects feasible transitions across successive time layers.","This is useful when future occupancy can be supplied as reservations. Silver’s cooperative pathfinding paper develops this idea for multiple agents. Applying it to Snake requires an extra argument: the snake’s future occupancy is generated by the same actions being planned."]},{"heading":"Release-time planning is an approximation","paragraphs":["A current body provides nominal release times under a no-growth assumption. A planner can reject arrivals earlier than the recorded release time. This captures one important constraint that static search misses.","But two paths reaching the same cell at the same time may leave different body trails. Merging them into one (cell,time) vertex discards information that can determine the legality of the next action. A time coordinate by itself does not repair an incomplete state."]},{"heading":"Search the state that determines legality","paragraphs":["An exact forward search can store the ordered body, direction, food, and any pending growth at each node. Each outgoing edge invokes the transition function. States may be merged only when they are equivalent for the future task being solved.","This state space is far larger than the board. A practical planner may cap depth, cap memory, or use a beam. Each cap changes the guarantee: failure to find a route under a budget is not proof that no route exists."],"formula":"node = (ordered body, direction, food, rule state, time if needed)"},{"heading":"Choose the guarantee before the representation","paragraphs":["If the goal is a cheap warning signal, a release-time approximation may be appropriate. If the goal is validating a proposed short action sequence, exact simulation is inexpensive. If the goal is proving that every continuation fails, exhaustive state reasoning or a separate theorem is needed.","In experiments, separate “no path in the abstraction,” “budget exhausted,” and “proven dead under the exact model.” These outcomes mean different things. The lab’s pathfinding experiment deliberately uses a static graph, so its shortest-path label applies only to that frozen graph."]}],"takeaway":"Time helps only when the state also preserves the history needed to predict future occupancy.","sources":["silver-2005","gym-env"],"abstract":"Space-time search augments a cell with an arrival time to represent changing occupancy. In Snake, future occupied cells depend on the agent’s own actions and on growth, so a reservation schedule cannot generally be treated as fixed. We compare release-time approximations with full-body successor states and identify the guarantee each representation can support."},{"slug":"complexity","title":"State-space growth and computational complexity","category":"Foundations","level":"Advanced","description":"Distinguish graph size, body configurations, planning horizons, and hardness claims.","diagram":"search","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"The board is the small part","paragraphs":["A W by H board has N = WH cells and at most four neighbors per cell. Static breadth-first search on this graph is linear in the number of cells and edges. That makes finding an ordinary shortest route a modest computation.","Snake planning is different because the body changes the legal moves. Each ordered body configuration can be a different search state. Counting only the N possible head positions hides nearly all of this structure."]},{"heading":"A loose upper bound is still informative","paragraphs":["For a body of fixed length ℓ, choosing an ordered sequence of distinct cells gives at most N!/(N−ℓ)! possibilities. Most of these sequences are not legal connected bodies, so this is a deliberately loose upper bound, not an exact count.","A geometric bound starts with N choices for the head and at most four choices for each next segment; forbidding immediate backtracking gives at most N\xb74\xb73^(ℓ−2) for ℓ≥2. Boundaries and self-avoidance reduce it further. Both bounds illustrate why storing complete bodies can be expensive."],"formula":"# bodies ≤ min{N!/(N−ℓ)!, N\xb74\xb73^(ℓ−2)}    for ℓ ≥ 2"},{"heading":"A theorem needs the right problem","paragraphs":["Hamiltonian path and circuit problems on general grid graphs have established hardness results. An unobstructed rectangle is a special graph family with much more structure. It is incorrect to transfer a general-grid hardness statement to every rectangular instance without qualification.","It is also incorrect to treat a theorem about a Hamiltonian path as automatically proving a theorem about a stochastic Snake objective. The input, permitted actions, objective, and decision question must match. The classic grid paper is useful precisely because it distinguishes graph families and problems."]},{"heading":"Measure the actual computational burden","paragraphs":["For a planner, report expanded states, maximum frontier size, time per action, and the search budget. For a learned controller, report training resources separately from inference resources. A fast action selection does not imply cheap training.","Use the simplest representation that supports the desired claim. Snapshot search is suitable for static routing. Full-state search is appropriate for exact short-horizon legality. Structured policies such as cycle following can bypass large searches by maintaining a strong invariant under restrictive assumptions."]}],"takeaway":"Complexity belongs to a precisely defined problem. A small visual board can encode many ordered-body states.","sources":["itai-1982","nx-bfs"],"abstract":"A grid with N cells does not imply a Snake planning problem with only N states. Ordered body configurations and planning horizons expand the search space. We distinguish loose counting bounds from hardness results, explain why general grid Hamiltonicity does not settle the unobstructed rectangular case, and specify measurements that expose practical search costs."},{"slug":"engine-design","title":"Deterministic simulation and reproducible transitions","category":"Engineering","level":"Intermediate","description":"Separate transitions from rendering, make randomness explicit, and test the semantics.","diagram":"state","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"Make the transition function the center","paragraphs":["A reliable engine accepts a state and an action and returns the next state. Rendering, keyboard handling, and animation should sit outside this function. This separation allows the same rules to support interactive play, search, replay, and evaluation.","SnakeGuide’s lab uses integer coordinates, an ordered body array, a direction vector, one food cell, and explicit generator state. A collision ends the episode. Filling the last available cell produces a win, avoiding an infinite retry loop when spawning food."]},{"heading":"Use one logical clock","paragraphs":["The game advances by discrete ticks. Input is queued between ticks, and only one requested direction change is consumed per tick. Otherwise, two rapid turns can bypass a reversal check that was written for a single update.","Animation may interpolate between cells, but collision logic should remain tied to discrete states. A frame-rate-dependent engine can change behavior when a browser tab slows down. The visual frame clock and the rules clock are different concerns."]},{"heading":"Make food sampling explicit","paragraphs":["The lab enumerates free cells in row-major order and selects an index using a 32-bit linear congruential generator. The generator is deterministic and suitable for an educational replay; it is not a cryptographic generator. The modulo selection has slight distribution bias, so this demo should not be presented as a statistically exact uniform sampler.","Identical seed, action sequence, and engine version reproduce a run. The seed alone does not: different actions change when food is consumed and which cells are available. The exported JSON contains a state snapshot, rather than a complete action replay."],"formula":"ξ′ = (1664525\xb7ξ + 1013904223) mod 2\xb3\xb2"},{"heading":"Test properties that define the game","paragraphs":["After each valid transition, all body cells must be inside the board, consecutive segments must be adjacent, and distinct segments must occupy distinct cells. Length increases by one exactly when food is eaten. Newly placed food must not overlap the body.","Boundary tests should include tail entry, reversal requests, immediate growth, full-board completion, and collision. If exposing a learning API, distinguish task termination from an artificial time-limit truncation. That distinction affects learning targets and evaluation reports, as the Gymnasium API makes explicit."]}],"takeaway":"A reproducible engine is a specification you can execute. Rendering should not decide the rules.","sources":["gym-env","numpy-rng","battlesnake-rules"],"abstract":"Reproducibility begins with an explicit transition function. We separate simulation ticks from rendering, describe seeded food placement and its limitations, and enumerate invariants for collision, growth, and full-board completion. The reference implementation makes each rule inspectable; replayability additionally requires actions, rules, generator details, and event order."},{"slug":"strategy","title":"From local heuristics to policy-level evaluation","category":"Foundations","level":"Essential","description":"Translate “leave yourself room” into explicit assumptions, measurements, and failure cases.","diagram":"bottleneck","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"Name the decision you are making","paragraphs":["A player may describe a move as “safe” because it avoids immediate collision, preserves open space, or follows a familiar pattern. These are different claims. Begin by deciding whether the objective is immediate legality, eventual food collection, long survival, or full-board completion.","A route that minimizes steps to food may be poor for survival. A cycle policy can preserve a collision-free traversal yet take a long route to food. Strategy is partly the choice of objective, not only the choice of movement rule."]},{"heading":"Turn intuition into features","paragraphs":["“Leave yourself room” can become a reachable-area score. “Keep an exit” can become a test for a path to a chosen target. “Do not cut yourself off” can become a component or bottleneck analysis. Each translation is an abstraction with a specific scope.","Write down how a candidate action is simulated and how its resulting state is scored. If several moves tie, specify a deterministic ordering. Otherwise, two implementations with the same verbal strategy may behave differently."]},{"heading":"Construct counterexamples","paragraphs":["A spacious chamber with one entrance challenges area-based reasoning. Food at the end of a narrow corridor challenges greedy distance minimization. A body about to vacate a passage challenges a frozen obstacle model. Small examples reveal the assumptions behind a strategy more clearly than a single impressive run.","Save the complete ordered body, food, obstacles, and intended action. A screenshot alone may omit direction, growth state, or timing. The lab’s state export can preserve these details for its own rules, while other games need their own recording formats."]},{"heading":"Evaluate the claim you actually made","paragraphs":["For immediate collision avoidance, count illegal proposed actions. For survival, report termination causes and episode lengths. For food efficiency, report moves per food alongside completion and failure rates. No single score captures all three objectives.","Human speedrunning introduces additional constraints such as input speed, game version, and category rules. Algorithm demonstrations here use explicitly documented educational rules; they do not establish a leaderboard record or reproduce a particular competitive category. A useful technical guide keeps those interpretations separate."]}],"takeaway":"A strategy becomes testable when its objective, state features, action rule, and failure criterion are explicit.","sources":["nx-cut","tapsell-2015","agarwal-2021"],"abstract":"A local score is useful only when its intended claim is explicit. We translate space, distance, and escape intuitions into measurable features, construct failure cases, and distinguish legal-move checks from long-horizon policy evaluation. The resulting framework supports interpretable baselines without attributing survival guarantees to unverified heuristics."},{"slug":"variants","title":"A taxonomy of Snake rule variants","category":"Foundations","level":"Essential","description":"Compare classic Snake, wrapping boards, wall variants, and simultaneous multiplayer.","diagram":"learning","minutes":6,"sections":[{"heading":"Boundary topology changes distance","paragraphs":["On a solid rectangular board, reaching a cell on the opposite edge requires traversing the intervening columns. On a wrapping board, crossing the edge may be a single move. A heuristic derived for one topology can overestimate distance in the other.","For a W by H toroidal board without obstacles, the shortest four-neighbor distance uses the smaller wrapped displacement in each coordinate. Obstacles can only increase that distance, so it remains a lower bound for routing under the same motion rules."],"formula":"h = min(|Δx|, W−|Δx|) + min(|Δy|, H−|Δy|)"},{"heading":"Growth is part of the dynamics","paragraphs":["Immediate growth retains the tail on the food-eating move. Other games can store pending growth or duplicate the tail and reveal the extension later. These implementations change when cells become traversable.","Multiple food cells, changing food values, and timed obstacles add further state. The phrase “standard Snake” is not a complete environment specification. Store the precise rule parameters with every evaluation."]},{"heading":"Multiplayer introduces simultaneous decisions","paragraphs":["In simultaneous multiplayer, another snake’s next head position is not known when choosing an action. A cell empty in the current frame may be contested on the next turn. Modeling every opponent as a static wall omits this interaction.","Battlesnake documents a concrete ruleset with health, simultaneous movement, body collisions, and head-to-head resolution. Its official rules are a primary reference for that game. They should not be treated as a universal rulebook for all Snake variants."]},{"heading":"Compare methods on matched tasks","paragraphs":["A completion guarantee on a fixed empty rectangle cannot be transferred to changing obstacles, adversarial opponents, or a starvation limit. Similarly, an average score from one board size says little about another without normalization and the full experimental setup.","A comparison table should include board topology, initial length, food process, growth semantics, collision order, time limits, and opponent policy. Keep the software version or ruleset identifier. The linked Battlesnake ruleset object is a practical example of recording both names and versioned settings."]}],"takeaway":"Changing one rule can invalidate a heuristic, a proof, or a benchmark comparison.","sources":["battlesnake-rules","battlesnake-ruleset","nx-astar"],"abstract":"Boundary wrapping, growth timing, simultaneous opponents, and obstacle events change the computational task. We compare their effects on distance functions, collision resolution, and the state required for planning. A matched comparison must preserve the complete ruleset rather than assuming that implementations sharing the name Snake define the same environment."}]'),i=JSON.parse('[{"slug":"bfs","title":"Breadth-first search on static occupancy graphs","category":"Algorithms","level":"Intermediate","description":"Find a shortest food route, then examine what that route fails to prove.","minutes":6,"diagram":"search","sections":[{"heading":"Choose the graph before choosing the algorithm","paragraphs":["Represent each currently traversable cell by a vertex. Connect cells that share an edge, provided the rules allow that move. With unit movement cost, this graph captures the question: how many moves separate the head from a target while the blocked cells remain fixed? NetworkX\'s unweighted shortest-path interface solves this graph problem. The important qualification is the frozen obstacle model. Snake changes that model after every move.","For a simple baseline, remove the body except the head from the graph. A more permissive baseline may release the current tail when a nongrowing move removes it. Document that choice: the two graphs can return different routes for the same displayed board. Neither convention alone describes the entire future body."]},{"heading":"A queue creates a distance wave","paragraphs":["Breadth-first search uses a first-in, first-out queue. Discover the starting cell at distance zero, then discover its unvisited neighbors at distance one. Every later layer is one move farther away. Mark a vertex when it enters the queue, not when it leaves; otherwise several parents may enqueue the same cell and inflate the work.","Store a predecessor for each discovered cell. Once the food is reached, follow those predecessors backward and reverse the sequence. A four-neighbor board with N cells has at most four outgoing neighbor checks per cell, so a full traversal uses linear time and linear storage. A rectangular obstacle-free board has an especially useful sanity check: the returned distance equals the Manhattan distance."],"formula":"d(v) = d(parent(v)) + 1","code":"queue = [start]\\nparent = {start: None}\\nfor cell in queue:\\n    if cell == goal:\\n        break\\n    for nxt in neighbors(cell):\\n        if nxt not in blocked and nxt not in parent:\\n            parent[nxt] = cell\\n            queue.append(nxt)"},{"heading":"Shortest is local to the objective","paragraphs":["Consider two entrances to a pocket containing food. One takes four moves, the other six. BFS selects the four-move route if both are legal in its graph. But eating may leave the head facing a wall while its own body closes the only exit. The algorithm has answered its specified question correctly; the objective omitted the continuation.","Replanning after each move does not retroactively repair an irreversible entry into that pocket. A one-step controller can repeatedly select locally valid moves until no valid successor remains. The distinction is between reaching a target vertex in a snapshot and finding a sequence of complete game states that remains viable."]},{"heading":"Use the baseline to expose assumptions","paragraphs":["Inspect the returned path before executing it. Count its edges, confirm every edge is legal, and compare it with an independent distance calculation on small boards. Then replay the route through the actual transition function, including growth. If the replay fails, the graph abstraction was too permissive or the engine and planner disagree.","When BFS finds no static path, this does not necessarily establish that food is unreachable in the real game. Waiting through a legal detour may let the tail uncover a corridor. Conversely, finding a path says little about survival after the meal. These are concrete limits of the model, not shortcomings of the queue."]},{"heading":"Measure the useful things","paragraphs":["For an algorithm comparison, retain the same board, neighbor order, target, and blocking convention. Report path length, expanded cells, and decision latency separately. A shorter route and a cheaper search are different accomplishments. For a game evaluation, add food collected, failure cause, and the number of truncated episodes. A search diagram should make the frontier, explored cells, and final route distinguishable."]}],"takeaway":"BFS certifies a shortest path in the graph you give it. It does not certify the future safety of the snake.","sources":["nx-bfs","gym-env"],"abstract":"Breadth-first search provides a shortest route on an unweighted graph through FIFO expansion. We specify the graph induced by a frozen Snake board, trace the distance wave, and separate route optimality from post-growth safety. The interactive comparison makes the objective mismatch visible when entering some cells incurs additional cost."},{"slug":"astar","title":"A* search: admissibility, consistency, and state design","category":"Algorithms","level":"Intermediate","description":"Understand admissibility, tie-breaking, and the gap between efficient search and safe play.","minutes":6,"diagram":"search","sections":[{"heading":"Separate the cost paid from the cost predicted","paragraphs":["A* prioritizes a candidate using the cost already paid to reach it plus a heuristic estimate of the remaining cost. With h equal to zero, the priority becomes Dijkstra\'s. With a useful lower bound, the frontier tends to favor cells pointing toward the target. The foundational work by Hart, Nilsson, and Raphael establishes heuristic search as a principled graph-search method rather than an arbitrary attraction toward the goal.","For Snake, first specify what a node means. It may be a cell in a frozen board, a cell with an arrival time, or an entire ordered body configuration. The same priority formula can search these different spaces, but the meaning and cost of a successor change dramatically."],"formula":"f(n) = g(n) + h(n)"},{"heading":"Derive a heuristic from the movement rules","paragraphs":["On a nonwrapping four-neighbor board with unit step cost, Manhattan distance is a lower bound: every horizontal coordinate difference requires a horizontal move, and every vertical difference requires a vertical move. Obstacles cannot make the route shorter than this bound. For example, from (2, 3) to (7, 5), any route needs at least seven moves. A wall may make the true route longer.","This derivation is conditional. If the board wraps, moving across a boundary can shorten the route, so ordinary Manhattan distance may overestimate. If diagonal moves are permitted, its interpretation changes again. If every edge costs at least c, multiply the Manhattan lower bound by c; arbitrary risk bonuses do not justify using a larger multiplier."],"formula":"h(x,y) = |x − x_goal| + |y − y_goal|"},{"heading":"Admissibility is not the whole implementation","paragraphs":["A heuristic is admissible when it never exceeds the true remaining minimum cost. It is consistent when h(u) is at most the edge cost from u to v plus h(v), for every edge. Consistency supports settling nodes without later reopening them in the usual graph-search implementation. With an admissible but inconsistent heuristic, a correct implementation may need to reopen a node after discovering a better route.","NetworkX explicitly warns that overestimating heuristics can return nonshortest paths. Its implementation also caches a node\'s first heuristic value. If an estimate changes with the evolving snake state, encode that state in the node or restart the search. Updating a visual board while reusing stale search values is a different algorithm from static A*."]},{"heading":"Tie-breaking changes the picture","paragraphs":["Many grid cells share the same f value. Choosing larger g first often produces a more directed-looking expansion, whereas a different tie-break can spread the frontier. Both may return equally short paths. Therefore a visually narrow search is not, by itself, evidence of a superior heuristic. Fix tie-breaking before comparing expanded-node counts.","A priority queue may contain an obsolete entry after a better g value is found. Ignore entries whose stored cost no longer matches the best known cost. Keep predecessor updates aligned with cost improvements. These details matter more than adding visual motion to the frontier."]},{"heading":"Apply the guarantee to the correct problem","paragraphs":["A* on the static food graph optimizes that graph\'s route cost. It does not guarantee that growth will leave an escape route. A* over complete body states can model those transitions, but the state space is much larger and a finite planning horizon can still miss later traps. Report the search model, termination condition, expansion budget, and fallback move. If a budget is exhausted, label the result as incomplete rather than unreachable."]}],"takeaway":"A heuristic is useful only relative to explicit movement rules and a correctly implemented search state.","sources":["hart-1968","nx-astar","nx-dijkstra"],"abstract":"A* combines accumulated path cost with a lower bound on remaining cost. We derive Manhattan distance from four-neighbor movement, examine consistency and node reopening, and explain how tie-breaking changes expansion patterns. Guarantees apply to the chosen search state: a static food route, a timed path, and a complete body trajectory are different planning problems."},{"slug":"dijkstra","title":"Weighted shortest paths and risk-sensitive objectives","category":"Algorithms","level":"Intermediate","description":"Use weighted paths without confusing a penalty function with a survival probability.","minutes":6,"diagram":"search","sections":[{"heading":"A longer route may be cheaper","paragraphs":["Breadth-first search treats every move equally. Dijkstra\'s algorithm is useful when legal moves have different nonnegative costs. The goal is a path with minimum total cost, which may contain more steps than the shortest unweighted route. NetworkX\'s Dijkstra interface supports weights stored on edges or computed by a function.","Suppose a five-step route crosses two hazardous cells and a seven-step route avoids them. If a normal step costs one and entering a hazardous cell adds three, the first route costs eleven and the second costs seven. The detour is optimal for that cost model. It is not automatically optimal for score, survival, or wall-clock time."],"formula":"cost(P) = Σ cost(v_i, v_(i+1))"},{"heading":"Relaxation is the central operation","paragraphs":["Maintain the best known cost to each vertex. When expanding u, consider whether reaching a neighbor v through u improves its current value. If so, update the value and predecessor. A minimum-priority queue chooses the next unsettled vertex. With nonnegative edge weights, a removed minimum has no undiscovered cheaper route through a more expensive frontier vertex.","For a sparse grid, a binary-heap implementation typically takes O((V + E) log V) time. The precise bound depends on queue operations and how duplicate entries are handled. BFS is simpler when every edge has identical cost; replacing its queue with a heap then adds machinery without changing the optimization problem."],"formula":"d(v) ← min(d(v), d(u) + w(u,v))","code":"if distance[u] + weight(u, v) < distance[v]:\\n    distance[v] = distance[u] + weight(u, v)\\n    parent[v] = u\\n    push(priority_queue, (distance[v], v))"},{"heading":"Design costs that answer a defensible question","paragraphs":["A Snake planner might penalize proximity to a wall, entry into a narrow region, or a predicted collision threat. These penalties are design choices. A cost of five does not mean a five-percent death probability. If the weights are heuristic, describe them as heuristic and evaluate the resulting policy against alternatives.","Some costs cannot be represented by a cell alone. Charging for a turn requires the incoming direction, so the node should include direction. Charging for a corridor becoming occupied after several moves requires time or a richer state. If two arrivals at the same cell have different relevant futures, merging them can discard the better plan."]},{"heading":"Keep reward and route cost separate","paragraphs":["Do not insert a negative edge whenever food is encountered and expect ordinary Dijkstra to remain valid. Negative costs break its settling argument, and negative cycles may make a minimum undefined. A reward-maximizing sequential game is not automatically a shortest-path problem. Define a finite objective or use an algorithm suited to the resulting model.","Even nonnegative risk penalties can conceal hard constraints. An actually illegal move should be absent from the graph, not merely expensive. Otherwise a planner may select it when all legal routes cost more. Conversely, treating every mildly risky cell as forbidden can prevent useful escape routes."]},{"heading":"Audit weighted results","paragraphs":["Display both route length and total weighted cost. Show the contribution of each penalty so readers can explain why a detour was chosen. Check the algorithm against BFS when all weights are one and against manually computed tiny examples when weights differ. Repeat sensitivity experiments with several penalty scales. If a small coefficient change reverses the policy, that sensitivity is a substantive result about the model."]}],"takeaway":"Dijkstra makes a weighted objective precise. The credibility of the resulting move still depends on the objective and state representation.","sources":["nx-dijkstra","nx-bfs"],"abstract":"Dijkstra’s algorithm finds minimum-cost paths under nonnegative edge weights. We derive relaxation, distinguish movement cost from a learned reward, and show why a manually chosen penalty is not a calibrated probability of failure. A shared weighted-grid example compares the fewest-move path with the minimum-cost path and exposes their different objectives."},{"slug":"hamiltonian-cycles","title":"Hamiltonian cycle invariants for collision avoidance","category":"Algorithms","level":"Advanced","description":"Why a board-spanning cycle can prevent self-collision, and exactly when the argument stops applying.","minutes":7,"diagram":"cycle","sections":[{"heading":"Replace repeated planning with a fixed order","paragraphs":["A Hamiltonian cycle visits every vertex exactly once before returning to its start. On a board of N traversable cells, label this cycle 0 through N − 1. Following it means repeatedly advancing to the next label modulo N. Tapsell\'s Snake implementation explains a practical use of this ordering, including shortcuts that preserve the relative order of body segments.","The basic version needs no food search. Every free food cell lies somewhere ahead on the cycle, so a snake that keeps following the cycle eventually reaches it. This trades route efficiency for a simple invariant. The food may be geometrically nearby while still far away in the chosen cyclic order."],"formula":"next(i) = (i + 1) mod N"},{"heading":"Parity tells you when a rectangle admits a cycle","paragraphs":["Color a four-neighbor rectangular grid like a chessboard. Every edge changes color, so every cycle alternates colors and has even length. A Hamiltonian cycle is therefore impossible when the board has an odd number of cells. A one-cell-wide nonwrapping board also cannot contain such a cycle under the ordinary simple-graph definition.","For an unobstructed m by n rectangle, a Hamiltonian cycle exists when both dimensions exceed one and at least one is even. A constructive route reserves an edge column as a return corridor and snakes through the remaining cells. The grid-graph literature distinguishes this structured case from general grids with missing vertices, where finding Hamilton paths and circuits is computationally harder."]},{"heading":"State the invariant before claiming safety","paragraphs":["Assume the initial body is a contiguous, consistently oriented segment of the cycle; every nongrowing step removes its tail; eating adds exactly one segment; there are no changing obstacles; and filling the board ends the episode. Before the board is full, the next cycle cell is outside that contiguous body. Moving the head forward and updating the tail preserves contiguity. Induction gives freedom from self-collision while these assumptions hold.","This argument is about the specified engine. A starvation timer may expire before distant food is reached. Newly spawned walls may sever the cycle. A different initial body can already violate its order. A cycle drawn on top of a screenshot is not sufficient evidence that the snake can safely join it."]},{"heading":"Shortcuts require a stronger check","paragraphs":["A shortcut can reach food sooner by leaving the next cycle edge while preserving cyclic body order. But geometric adjacency is not the only condition. The move must not overtake occupied body positions, and growth must not let the head catch the tail. A correct implementation needs a precise convention for cyclic distance and for whether the tail moves on the chosen step.","Before enabling shortcuts, test the pure cycle controller. Then compare a shortcut controller against a complete transition replay on adversarial states near the tail and near full occupancy. A successful animation on a sparse board exercises the easiest regime, where many incorrect shortcut rules appear safe."]},{"heading":"Interpret completion and efficiency separately","paragraphs":["A controller can reliably fill a compatible board and still take many moves per apple. Report completion rate and step cost separately. If the cycle is randomly generated, record its seed and construction algorithm. Never describe a fixed-cycle guarantee as a solution to every wall-mode instance: the moment the traversable graph changes, its certificate must be revalidated or replaced."]}],"takeaway":"A Hamiltonian policy is powerful because its safety follows from an invariant. Its assumptions belong beside the result.","sources":["itai-1982","tapsell-2015","battlesnake-rules"],"abstract":"A board-spanning cycle replaces repeated route selection with a fixed cyclic order. We state the rectangular parity condition and the body-order assumptions behind collision avoidance, then examine why shortcuts need additional checks. The interactive figure changes body length and head position while preserving the cyclic order; it is a geometric illustration rather than a food-completion experiment."},{"slug":"reinforcement-learning","title":"Value-based and policy-gradient methods for Snake","category":"Research","level":"Advanced","description":"Build a learning problem whose observations, actions, rewards, and evaluation correspond to the game you mean.","minutes":7,"diagram":"learning","sections":[{"heading":"Write the task before training the model","paragraphs":["A learning environment needs a state, an observation, an action set, a transition rule, and a reward. For Snake, the environment can retain the ordered body even if the agent sees only an image. Actions might be absolute directions or relative turns. These are not interchangeable interfaces: a relative action depends on current orientation, while an absolute action may include an illegal reversal.","Choose the evaluation target independently of the training reward. Maximizing discounted food reward, maximizing final length, and maximizing survival time can induce different behavior. A controller that circles forever may score well under a survival bonus while making no progress toward filling the board."]},{"heading":"DQN learns action values","paragraphs":["A Q network estimates expected discounted return for each action. A common temporal-difference target combines the observed reward with the largest predicted next-state value, except at true terminal states. Mnih and colleagues\' 2013 work learns action values from images and uses experience replay. Those Atari experiments motivate an algorithmic approach; they are not evidence that a particular Snake implementation works.","In a Snake experiment, replay records should retain the observation, selected action, reward, successor observation, and termination information. Check them by decoding several transitions back into board states. A single coordinate transposition can produce plausible loss curves while training on the wrong movement relationship."],"formula":"y = r + γ(1 − terminal) max_a Q_target(o_next, a)"},{"heading":"PPO updates a policy directly","paragraphs":["PPO uses trajectories to update a parameterized action distribution. Its clipped surrogate limits the incentive to change a sampled action\'s probability ratio too far in one update. This is an optimization mechanism, not a guarantee that the policy avoids collisions. The original paper describes a family of policy-gradient methods and evaluates them on several control benchmarks.","For Snake, an action mask can eliminate moves known to be immediately illegal. Apply the same mask when sampling actions and calculating their probabilities during training. Otherwise the logged policy probability and the behavior that generated the data disagree. State whether the baseline controller receives the same legality information; a comparison can be distorted by unequal interfaces."]},{"heading":"Do not let episode bookkeeping redefine the task","paragraphs":["Gymnasium separates termination from truncation. A collision or a declared full-board win can be a true terminal event. A diagnostic step limit may instead truncate an otherwise continuing task. Value targets should reflect that distinction; treating every cutoff as death changes the learning problem.","A finite-horizon task is different: if the rules genuinely end after a fixed number of moves, the remaining time belongs in a fully observed state. Write down whether a maximum-step setting is part of the game or merely an experimental budget. The same numeric cutoff can have either meaning."]},{"heading":"Make improvement falsifiable","paragraphs":["Compare against random legal play, a static path planner, and a compatible cycle policy where applicable. Hold evaluation seeds apart from tuning seeds. Record multiple independent training runs, not just many games from one favorable model. Plot the distribution of final length and report collision causes and decision cost. Without measured runs, describe the learning system as a design or implementation, not a trained result.","Inspect counterexamples: the first missed reachable apple, the first avoidable pocket entry, and the first action-mask disagreement often explain more than a smooth average-reward curve. A useful research artifact connects its numerical result back to a reproducible sequence of states."]}],"takeaway":"The neural network is only one component. Observation semantics, episode boundaries, and evaluation determine what learning success means.","sources":["mnih-2013","schulman-2017","gym-env","henderson-2018"],"abstract":"A learning formulation must specify observations, actions, transitions, rewards, and episode boundaries. We connect DQN’s action-value learning and PPO’s policy updates to that formulation, with attention to termination, truncation, and evaluation. The cited papers establish general methods; their reported results do not measure the Snake environment implemented here."},{"slug":"reward-shaping","title":"Potential-based reward shaping and policy invariance","category":"Research","level":"Advanced","description":"Distinguish useful training signals from incentives that reward the wrong behavior.","minutes":7,"diagram":"learning","sections":[{"heading":"An intuitive bonus defines a new incentive","paragraphs":["Suppose the intended task rewards food and penalizes collision. Adding a small reward for every surviving step can make endless circling attractive. Rewarding every move toward food can encourage repeated approach-and-retreat patterns if retreat is not charged consistently. These are direct consequences of the return calculation, not evidence that an agent has misunderstood its instructions.","Audit a proposed reward by constructing short trajectories before training. Compare a direct meal, a safe detour, a useless loop, and an early collision. Sum the discounted rewards explicitly. If the wrong trajectory already wins this calculation, additional compute will not fix the objective."]},{"heading":"Use a potential when policy preservation matters","paragraphs":["Ng, Harada, and Russell identify potential-based shaping as a policy-preserving transformation under their stated MDP assumptions. Add F(s,s′) = γΦ(s′) − Φ(s) to the base reward, using the same discount γ as the task. Along a trajectory, the shaping return telescopes to −Φ(s₀) + γ^TΦ(s_T). For bounded potentials and discount below one, the final term vanishes in the infinite-horizon limit. For an episodic construction, setting terminal potential to zero removes the endpoint dependence.","This is a structural guarantee about the objective, not a guarantee of faster learning or finite-sample convergence. Arbitrary distance bonuses do not qualify merely because they appear to encourage progress."],"formula":"r_shaped(s,a,s′) = r_base(s,a,s′) + γΦ(s′) − Φ(s)"},{"heading":"A Snake potential has edge cases","paragraphs":["A candidate potential is negative distance to the current food. On a finite board, Manhattan distance is bounded and easy to calculate. However, the goal changes after eating. Compute Φ on the actual successor state, including newly spawned food, rather than silently retaining the old target. The resulting jump can look surprising while still being consistent with a state potential.","If no food remains after a full-board win, define the terminal potential explicitly instead of calling a distance function with a missing target. If a path-based potential uses infinity for unreachable food, it is no longer bounded; replace that with a documented finite value or choose another potential. An implementation detail can invalidate the assumptions behind the formula."]},{"heading":"Keep training signals visible","paragraphs":["Log base reward and shaping reward separately. A rising shaped return may reflect different state potentials rather than more food. Evaluate the final policy on the original game metrics, and retain the shaping configuration as part of the experiment. The evaluation code should not need to reverse-engineer what a large aggregate reward means.","For a concrete unit-level audit, choose a fixed sequence of valid states and compare the explicit discounted sum of shaping terms with the telescoping expression. Test sequences containing a food respawn, a collision, and a full-board terminal state. These checks examine mathematical consistency rather than asserting that training will succeed."]},{"heading":"Do not confuse truncation with a terminal boundary","paragraphs":["A training rollout can stop before the task ends. Forcing the final potential to zero at every arbitrary rollout boundary changes the shaping calculation unless the continuing value is handled consistently. Decide whether a limit ends the actual game or only the collected sample. Then use that decision in both the return target and potential treatment.","Potential shaping remains a design choice. A simple sparse reward may be adequate for a small controlled experiment. The reason to introduce a potential is a specified learning problem, accompanied by an audit that preserves the evaluation objective."]}],"takeaway":"Calculate the incentives on short trajectories. When using potential shaping, preserve its discount and boundary assumptions in code.","sources":["ng-1999","gym-env"],"abstract":"Additional rewards can change the task an agent optimizes. We introduce potential-based shaping and its telescoping structure, then analyze discounting, terminal potentials, and finite episode boundaries. A distance bonus inherits no policy-preservation guarantee unless its construction satisfies the relevant assumptions; training signals should therefore remain separately observable."},{"slug":"reproducible-evaluation","title":"Experimental design for reproducible Snake evaluation","category":"Research","level":"Advanced","description":"Specify rules, randomness, failure metrics, and uncertainty before declaring one controller better.","minutes":7,"diagram":"state","sections":[{"heading":"Define the experiment, not just its name","paragraphs":["A result labeled Snake score is underspecified. Record board dimensions, boundary behavior, initial body, reversal rules, food count and placement policy, growth timing, obstacle schedule, win condition, and any step limit. A change to one field can alter which strategies are feasible. Include a versioned engine or commit identifier so the transition function can be recovered.","Battlesnake\'s API illustrates this principle by carrying a ruleset name, version, and settings. For a custom single-player engine, provide an equivalent configuration record. Do not infer that two programs implement identical rules simply because both draw a green snake and red food."]},{"heading":"A seed is necessary metadata, not a complete recipe","paragraphs":["Record the pseudorandom generator, seed list, generator version, and the sequence in which random choices are requested. NumPy\'s compatibility policy explicitly conditions reproducible streams on details beyond a seed. A saved sequence of food placements and actions can be more robust for debugging than expecting a future software stack to regenerate it.","Using the same seed across controllers does not guarantee the same food cells: different bodies can leave different valid spawn sets. Describe the coupling precisely. For instance, use the same random draw stream with a fixed ordering of free cells. This pairs the random inputs while allowing the realized game states to diverge."]},{"heading":"Separate the sources of variation","paragraphs":["For a deterministic planner, repeated environment seeds measure variation in game instances. For a learned controller, training initialization and data order introduce another layer. Many evaluation episodes from one trained model do not substitute for several independent training runs. Henderson and colleagues emphasize the reproducibility problems created by such experimental variation.","Retain training seeds and evaluation seeds separately. Tune parameters on a development set, then freeze them before evaluating held-out seeds. If the same difficult boards repeatedly guide improvements, those boards have become part of development even when the file is called test. Maintain a fresh evaluation set for the final comparison."]},{"heading":"Report distributions and failures","paragraphs":["Food collected, final occupancy, moves per food, completion rate, and decision latency answer different questions. Report collision categories and truncations rather than hiding them inside a single average. A controller that survives until the step cap without eating should remain visible as a stalled run.","Agarwal and colleagues show why limited-run RL comparisons need uncertainty estimates rather than point estimates alone. Choose intervals and resampling units that match the design. If comparing learned methods, preserve the hierarchy of training runs and episodes; if comparing fixed planners on paired boards, retain that pairing. A narrow interval from incorrectly treating dependent observations as independent is misleading."]},{"heading":"Publish enough to replay a counterexample","paragraphs":["A useful result bundle contains the configuration, algorithm version, seeds, per-episode metrics, and replay traces for representative failures. Include exact commands and a machine-readable schema. Preserve an action\'s requested direction separately from the engine\'s applied move if invalid inputs are normalized. This allows a reviewer to determine whether a failure came from planning, action handling, or simulation.","Every chart should identify whether it shows measured runs, a constructed example, or an illustrative diagram. Until experiments have actually been executed, a benchmark page should present a protocol and runnable tools rather than fabricated performance numbers."]}],"takeaway":"A credible comparison is a recoverable experiment: rules, code, randomness, raw outcomes, and uncertainty travel together.","sources":["battlesnake-ruleset","numpy-rng","henderson-2018","agarwal-2021","gym-env"],"abstract":"A comparison requires complete rules, explicit randomness, and a defined sampling unit. We distinguish variation across training runs from repeated games by one trained model, specify failure and efficiency metrics, and discuss distributional reporting. The protocol emphasizes reconstructable counterexamples and qualified claims rather than a leaderboard based only on a mean score."},{"slug":"observation-design","title":"Observation sufficiency and the Markov property","category":"Research","level":"Advanced","description":"Body order, pending growth, and hidden state determine whether an observation supports reliable decisions.","minutes":7,"diagram":"state","sections":[{"heading":"The simulator state and agent observation differ","paragraphs":["The simulator may store an ordered list of body cells, direction, food locations, obstacles, pending growth, and rule-specific counters. The agent receives an observation derived from that state. A screenshot is one possible observation; a tensor of semantic board channels is another. Neither is automatically complete.","A fully informative observation must retain the variables needed to predict the next-state distribution after an action. If a finite game ends when a clock expires, the remaining clock matters. If growth is delayed, the growth state matters. If future food is sampled independently according to a known distribution, the agent need not see the random-number generator\'s internal bits to model that distribution."]},{"heading":"Occupancy alone can merge different futures","paragraphs":["An occupied-cell mask says where the body is but not necessarily the order in which cells will be released. Consider a body that touches itself without crossing: adjacency in the displayed occupied region does not uniquely identify the body\'s consecutive segments. Two valid ordered bodies can therefore have indistinguishable occupancy while their future tail releases differ.","Adding head and tail channels improves the representation but does not, in general, resolve every interior ordering ambiguity. An ordered coordinate list is explicit. A board channel that encodes each segment\'s position along the body also preserves the order, provided repeated positions permitted by a variant are handled separately. Battlesnake\'s initial overlapping segments illustrate why a universal single-value-per-cell encoding needs care."]},{"heading":"Compact features trade convenience for information","paragraphs":["A small vector containing immediate danger, current direction, and the direction of food can be convenient for a first agent. It can also map a wide-open board and a nearly sealed pocket to the same observation. A policy using that vector must choose the same action distribution for both unless it has memory. No larger feed-forward network can reconstruct information that the encoding discarded.","This is a testable limitation. Construct two board states with identical feature vectors but different safe continuations. If such a pair exists, the representation aliases those states. The experiment identifies a representational constraint, not necessarily a defect in the optimizer. Recurrent memory may help, but only when the observation history contains the missing information."]},{"heading":"Use channels with precise semantics","paragraphs":["For a structured board input, separate walls, food, head, body, and any hazards. Specify the coordinate origin and which tensor axis represents width. Encode orientation and scalar counters explicitly. If board sizes vary, define padding and a valid-cell mask so a padded zero cannot be mistaken for traversable space.","Rotation augmentation requires transforming actions as well as boards. A north move in the original state becomes a different move after rotation. Test the transition correspondence: transforming a successor should agree with stepping the transformed state using the transformed action. A failure exposes an implementation inconsistency before it becomes a training anomaly."]},{"heading":"Choose a research question the observation can support","paragraphs":["Pixel-based learning asks an agent to infer useful visual structure; semantic channels provide some of that structure in advance. DQN\'s original image-based experiments illustrate the former approach, but do not make it obligatory. Compare controllers with matching access to state, or disclose the difference as part of the experiment.","Gymnasium formalizes observation spaces and environment interfaces; it does not prove that an observation is sufficient. Document exactly what is exposed, hidden, normalized, and remembered. Then use state-pair counterexamples and replay inspection to establish the practical limits of the representation."]}],"takeaway":"Before increasing model capacity, verify that the observation retains the information the decision requires.","sources":["gym-env","mnih-2013","battlesnake-rules"],"abstract":"An observation can merge simulator states whose legal successors differ. We examine information lost by occupancy-only images and compact local features, including body order and pending growth. Channel definitions must be tied to the transition semantics; a recurrent controller may infer hidden state, but recurrence alone does not establish observability or a safety guarantee."}]'),r=JSON.parse('[{"id":"nx-bfs","title":"Single-source shortest paths","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/generated/networkx.algorithms.shortest_paths.unweighted.single_source_shortest_path.html","authors":"NetworkX developers","year":null,"type":"Official documentation","claim":"Unweighted shortest paths from one source to reachable vertices.","caution":"A path in a static occupancy graph is not a proof of survival in a moving-body game.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn4view0"},{"id":"nx-dijkstra","title":"Dijkstra path","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/generated/networkx.algorithms.shortest_paths.weighted.dijkstra_path.html","authors":"NetworkX developers","year":null,"type":"Official documentation","claim":"Minimum-weight graph paths and programmable edge weights.","caution":"Use nonnegative edge costs for the ordinary settled-node Dijkstra algorithm.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn4view1"},{"id":"hart-1968","title":"A Formal Basis for the Heuristic Determination of Minimum Cost Paths","url":"https://www.cs.auckland.ac.nz/courses/compsci709s2c/resources/Mike.d/astarNilsson.pdf","authors":"Peter E. Hart, Nils J. Nilsson, Bertram Raphael","year":1968,"type":"Research paper","claim":"Foundational heuristic graph search and A*.","caution":"State heuristic assumptions; avoid claiming A* is always faster or inherently safe for Snake.","verification":"PDF opened successfully; searchable text unavailable","ref":"turn5view1"},{"id":"nx-astar","title":"A* path","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/generated/networkx.algorithms.shortest_paths.astar.astar_path.html","authors":"NetworkX developers","year":null,"type":"Official documentation","claim":"A* API; an overestimating heuristic can lose shortest-path guarantees.","caution":"Reopening or heuristic consistency matters in graph search implementations.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn5view6"},{"id":"itai-1982","title":"Hamilton Paths in Grid Graphs","url":"https://csaws.cs.technion.ac.il/~itai/publications/Algorithms/Hamilton-paths.pdf","authors":"Alon Itai, Christos H. Papadimitriou, Jayme Luiz Szwarcfiter","year":1982,"type":"Research paper","claim":"Grid bipartiteness, rectangular Hamilton paths, and NP-completeness for general grid Hamilton paths/circuits.","caution":"Do not transfer the hardness claim for general grids to unobstructed rectangles. An m by n rectangle has a Hamiltonian cycle iff m,n>1 and mn is even.","verification":"PDF opened successfully","ref":"turn5view0"},{"id":"tapsell-2015","title":"Nokia 6110 Part 3 — Algorithms","url":"https://johnflux.com/2015/05/02/nokia-6110-part-3-algorithms/","authors":"John Tapsell","year":2015,"type":"Author implementation note","claim":"Practical Snake control using Hamiltonian tours and order-preserving shortcuts.","caution":"An implementation account, not a theorem about every Snake variant. Growth and order constraints must be checked.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn4view7"},{"id":"nx-cut","title":"Articulation points","url":"https://networkx.org/documentation/stable/reference/algorithms/generated/networkx.algorithms.components.articulation_points.html","authors":"NetworkX developers","year":null,"type":"Official documentation","claim":"A cut vertex increases the number of connected components when removed.","caution":"A cut in a frozen free-space graph need not remain a cut as the tail moves.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn4view2"},{"id":"silver-2005","title":"Cooperative Pathfinding","url":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AIIDE/article/view/18726","authors":"David Silver","year":2005,"type":"Research paper","claim":"Cooperative A* searches space-time and uses reservations for moving agents.","caution":"Transfer to Snake is an analogy. Snake occupancy depends on its own future actions and growth. Publisher search indexed successfully but direct opening hit bot protection.","verification":"Publisher record verified via search; direct access blocked by bot protection","ref":"turn6search3"},{"id":"mnih-2013","title":"Playing Atari with Deep Reinforcement Learning","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.5602","authors":"Volodymyr Mnih et al.","year":2013,"type":"Research paper","claim":"Learning action values from visual observations with deep Q-learning and experience replay.","caution":"Atari evidence does not establish Snake performance; this 2013 version should not be confused with the 2015 Nature paper.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn4view3"},{"id":"schulman-2017","title":"Proximal Policy Optimization Algorithms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1707.06347","authors":"John Schulman, Filip Wolski, Prafulla Dhariwal, Alec Radford, Oleg Klimov","year":2017,"type":"Research paper","claim":"Policy gradient learning through surrogate objectives, including a clipped probability-ratio objective.","caution":"PPO clipping is an optimization device, not a collision-safety certificate or universal superiority result.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn4view4"},{"id":"ng-1999","title":"Policy Invariance Under Reward Transformations: Theory and Application to Reward Shaping","url":"https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~russell/papers/icml99-shaping.pdf","authors":"Andrew Y. Ng, Daishi Harada, Stuart Russell","year":1999,"type":"Research paper","claim":"Potential-based reward shaping preserves optimal policies under the theorem\'s MDP and boundary assumptions.","caution":"Handle terminal potentials, discount factors, and truncation consistently; arbitrary distance bonuses do not inherit this guarantee.","verification":"PDF opened successfully","ref":"turn7view0"},{"id":"henderson-2018","title":"Deep Reinforcement Learning That Matters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.06560","authors":"Peter Henderson, Riashat Islam, Philip Bachman, Joelle Pineau, Doina Precup, David Meger","year":2018,"type":"Research paper","claim":"Randomness, implementation choices and reporting affect reproducibility in deep RL.","caution":"ArXiv first appeared in 2017; the conference version is AAAI 2018.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn4view5"},{"id":"agarwal-2021","title":"Deep Reinforcement Learning at the Edge of the Statistical Precipice","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.13264","authors":"Rishabh Agarwal, Max Schwarzer, Pablo Samuel Castro, Aaron Courville, Marc G. Bellemare","year":2021,"type":"Research paper","claim":"Interval estimates, performance profiles and robust aggregation help evaluate limited-run RL experiments.","caution":"Bootstrap units must match the experimental sampling hierarchy; repeated games from one trained model are not independent training runs.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn4view6"},{"id":"gym-env","title":"Gymnasium Env API","url":"https://gymnasium.farama.org/api/env/","authors":"Farama Foundation","year":null,"type":"Official documentation","claim":"Environment reset/step contracts, seeding, and the distinction between termination and truncation.","caution":"Custom Snake rules and observation completeness must be implemented separately; API compliance is not a correctness proof.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn5view5"},{"id":"numpy-rng","title":"Random-number compatibility policy","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/random/compatibility.html","authors":"NumPy developers","year":null,"type":"Official documentation","claim":"Reproducing pseudorandom streams requires matching more than a seed, including generator, method calls and environment.","caution":"Do not promise version-independent reproducibility from a seed alone.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn5view7"},{"id":"battlesnake-rules","title":"Battlesnake Game Rules","url":"https://docs.battlesnake.com/rules","authors":"Battlesnake","year":null,"type":"Official rules","claim":"Simultaneous movement, collision resolution, health and delayed visible growth through tail duplication.","caution":"Battlesnake is a specific multiplayer variant; its rules are not universal Snake rules.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn5view3"},{"id":"battlesnake-ruleset","title":"Battlesnake Ruleset API object","url":"https://docs.battlesnake.com/api/objects/ruleset","authors":"Battlesnake","year":null,"type":"Official documentation","claim":"Requests identify ruleset name, version and settings.","caution":"A reproducible evaluation must retain these fields rather than reporting only a game name.","verification":"Opened successfully","ref":"turn5view4"}]'),s=[...n,...i].map(e=>({...e,minutes:Math.ceil(e.sections.reduce((e,a)=>e+a.paragraphs.join(" ").split(/\s+/).length,0)/180)+2})),o=r}}]);